# The HAND-WRITTEN ceiling. conformance/gates fails if the number of COVERED rows whose ONLY
# artifact is a hand-written class in conformance/jls RISES above it.
#
# Why this needs a gate of its own. The UNCOVERED ratchet cannot see this at all: a row carried
# by a hand-written program is COVERED, so it is subtracted from the number that is watched. 50
# rows sat in exactly that position while every gate stayed green, and on 2026-07-27 that let
# "chapter 4 closes" be said out loud while six of chapter 4's rows were carried by the class
# crisp-tallying-chapters §4 forbids:
#
#   "This applies to the corpus, not to a subset chosen at authoring time. I twice proposed
#    restricting stitching to the 'genuinely combinatorial' chapters and hand-writing the rest;
#    that is the same scope-narrowing as cutting the generator outright, and it is not an
#    authoring decision."
#
# And §5 says what to do about a rule nobody enforces: "A rule that cannot fire is not a gate --
# the same defect this project keeps finding in its own tests."
#
# It is not a hard zero, because the debt is real and cannot clear in one commit. It is a
# number that may only FALL. A row leaves the set when a GENERATED artifact claims its section,
# which is what migrating a chapter into templates does.
#
# THE TESTS THIS DISTRUSTS ARE NOT HYPOTHETICALLY BAD. §15.24 was COVERED by a hand-written
# Ch15 plus five generated cases and still had three wrong answers -- it rejected two legal
# programs and accepted one the spec calls a compile-time error -- because every one of those
# artifacts exercised the conditional operator and none exercised its TYPE RULE. §8.3.1.2,
# §6.6.2 and §6.4.2 were each the same shape: a section with a test, a green suite, and a rule
# nobody had read off the page.
#
# LOWER it by migrating a chapter's rows into templates written FROM THE SPEC. Porting the
# hand-written assertions forward does not count and is worse than leaving them: it launders
# the blind spot into the generator, which then certifies it.
#
# 50 (this commit): the starting debt, across chapters 3, 4, 5, 10, 11, 14 and 15.
50
